      "chunk_id": "1d5b0ffe62ad9280:00011",
      "distance": 0.6809279918670654,
      "path_rel": "rhetorical_ontology/Rickert, Thomas - Towards Ecosophy in a Participating World-Rhetoric and Cosmology in Heidegger's Fourfold and Empedocles' Four Roots_(2018)_[My Copy].pdf",
      "page_start": 18,
      "page_end": 18,
      "text": "Empedocles and Heidegger continually emphasized the need to forge a positive way forward, to work not just with who people are but with where they are. We cannot escape the infrastructures, industries, and lifeways people currently have but must work within and through them towards a more ecologically attuned dwelling. In this regard, science, technology, and capitalism will need to become part of that path, despite the fact that these are some of the most common targets for environmentalist critique. On these points I take inspiration from Peter Sloterdijk's digression on spaceships in his massive philosophical trilogy, Spheres. Sloterdijk argues that spaceships are more than technological objects—they are equally ontological interventions. The suturing\n\nof a thing to ontology harkens back to Heidegger, and we can see how Sloterdijk's spaceship updates Heidegger's jugs and bridges. But there is also an inversion: jugs and bridges evoke an interconnected whole to which we are all wedded and indebted; a spaceship, however, operates in a realm of extreme inhospitality. Space is nonconductive for nearly all life as we know it. Since no aspect of dwelling in space can be taken for granted, a spaceship \"constitutes a world model,\" a kind of \"immanence machine.\"73 The level of explicatory detail necessary for surviving in such a harsh environment is vertiginous. To give one small example: astronauts must sleep with fans next to them, less their exhalations, which do not move in zero gravity, suffocate them in a bubble of nitrogen and carbon dioxide.74 There is no resting on unexplicated assumptions, as is possible back on Earth where the ecological background can simply be assumed, as Moore exemplifed through capitalism's cavalier appropriations of nature. Space lacks such \"ontological comfort.\"75\n\nThe point, Sloterdijk argues, is that \"space travel amounts to an ontological alphabetization: what follows from it is that the elements of being-in-the-world can, and must, be written.\"76 Sloterdijk picks up the thread we saw connecting Empedocles and Aristotle in the movement from roots to elements, where increasing abstraction and alphabetization formalize as science and episteme. Sloterdijk also counters Abram's argument that abstraction goes hand in hand with the West's loss of intimacy with the environment. Rather, Sloterdijk taps a great affrmation resonant with Empedocles and Heidegger. It is the affrmation of the fourfold. The lesson is that abstraction and alphabetization also gather the fourfold and disclose the worldly depths of rich interconnection. Spaceships necessitate this insight. For life to maintain itself in space, bubbles of lifeworld must be constructed. To construct a bubble of lifeworld requires a profoundly rich explication of an ecological background we take for granted on earth, where our lifeworld is simply given. For spacecraft, these ABCs of lifeworlding must be spelled out and implemented through the technics of disclosure and building. As Heidegger has argued, to build is to dwell, and here we see how this manifests in even the most focused technological enterprise.77\n\nThe explicative disclosure necessary for space travel thus affrms some of environmentalism's biggest demons—abstraction, science, technology, capitalist progress. But this disclosive work pushes through their dangers and lights up their roles in the ecological lifeworld it is claimed they despoil. Still, it may be argued that space travel is a feeting pursuit",
